WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.680
Sometimes Riot makes mistakes.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:09.520
There are dozens of times they've broken League of Legends by

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:14.140
accidentally creating something so hilariously busted and overpowered it dominates the game

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:15.960
for a week or two until they patch it out.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:20.440
But in 2015 Riot went into Worlds with a patch full of those kinds of mistakes.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:24.360
And the fallout has changed the way they've treated Worlds ever since.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:37.260
In August 2015, Riot re-worked a handful of champions to create a new class called Juggernauts.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:41.580
The idea was to have a few, mostly top-lane champs, be glaciers in lane.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:45.680
Incredibly powerful, hard to kill and very slow.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:51.620
Riot re-worked Darius, Garen, Mordekaiser and Skarner, who was actually so busted that Riot

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:54.220
hotfixed him six days after the patch dropped.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.040
All of the champions were tweaked here and there, but by the time Worlds rolled

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:03.680
around in October, Darius was pretty strong, and Mordekaiser was comically busted.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.520
"Mordekaiser's main strength is just being able to snowball the early game."

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.720
"He's bringing magic damage. He's a really good diver early on 2v1."

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:15.360
Darius’s power came in part from the damage and healing on his Decimate ability,

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.060
combined with the nasty execute on his ultimate.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.060
If an enemy got low enough, Darius could dunk them and kill them,

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.300
which actually reset his ultimate letting him do it again.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.560
That led to some unbelievable penta kills.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:39.440
(Casting)

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.940
Meanwhile, Mordekaiser’s big change was that Riot wanted him to be played

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:44.080
in the bot lane.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:48.840
Which was tricky, because Mordekaiser is a melee champ, and all of the AD carries he’d lane

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:49.960
against were ranged.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:55.180
To offset that, Riot made it so that Mordekaiser gained 100 percent of  experience for every minion

00:01:55.189 --> 00:01:58.000
he killed, instead of sharing it with his support partner.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.180
That meant that Mordekaiser hit level six when you were around level four.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:03.360
It wasn’t fun.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:15.840
(Casting)

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.300
Darius had a 72.6 pick/ban rate by the end of the tournament, making

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.880
it into 38 games with a 50 percent win rate.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:28.680
Mordekaiser on the other hand had a nearly 100 percent pick/ban rate, and a 100 percent win

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:33.120
rate across all four games he made it into.You might be wondering how Darius made it into

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:34.380
so many games.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:39.040
That’s because the Juggernauts weren’t the only balance problem with the Worlds 2015 patch.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:42.460
Next came Gangplank, who was re -worked a few months before Worlds.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:45.440
But was still in a pretty powerful place going into the tournament.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:49.880
He was a late-game monster with super safe laning, letting him farm easily and build

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:54.420
items that turned him into a teamfighting machine. Gangplank was able to deal huge damage

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:57.040
to most champions if they walked into his barrels.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:01.680
And if they survived that onslaught, his ultimate did enough damage to kill them in teamfights anyways.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:07.300
Gangplank had a 100% pick ban rate. Winning all four games he made it into.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:10.960
And of course, there were even more problems.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.120
The jungle was pretty low in diversity in patch 5.18

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:16.680
And there were only really three competitive junglers.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.820
Elise, Rek’Sai and Gragas.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.020
Unfortunately, Gragas was disabled partway through the tournament, after a bug found during a

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:28.020
Fnatic vs. EDG quarterfinals game was deemed grame breaking by Riot.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:32.920
This wasn’t a deliberate balance decision that Riot made, like Gangplank or Mordekaiser,

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:35.840
but it did negatively impact the rest of the tournament.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:42.180
"The issue that appeared in-game prevented Reignover from casting the Gragas Q spell and the refereeing team

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:48.200
they did investigate and exhaust every single option to resolve the issue in game.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:52.020
Including allowing Gragas to die and attempt to resolve the matter."

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:56.960
Elise and Rek’Sai were the only two viable junglers left after Gragas was disabled.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.960
Combined with Gangplank and the Juggernauts, it all meant that red side was at a hilarious

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:04.320
disadvantage throughout the entire tournament.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:05.960
Back to the stats.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:13.140
Elise had a 97.3 percent pick/ban rate, while Rek’sai came in with a 76.7% pick/ban rate.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:17.920
This was before League of Legends had 10 bans, which meant that red side was screwed.

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:22.440
They had to ban Mordekaiser and Gangplank, as well as one of the two main junglers

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:26.780
since not doing that gave the blue side the advantage of getting to pick the jungler they preferred

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:28.060
and banning the other.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.840
Blue side, on the other hand, got their pick of the litter.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:34.740
They usually banned Lulu, since she was also pretty powerful on that patch, but

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:35.840
otherwise?

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:41.480
They got to pick any strong champions red side left up, and they got to target players on the red side.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:46.920
Think about it this way, in their grand finals series against SK Telecom T1, the Koo Tigers

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:48.580
couldn't target Faker at all.

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:52.840
They didn’t ban a single mid lane champion all series, even in games where they were on the blue

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:57.500
side, because they had their hands too full banning out OP top laners and junglers.

00:04:57.660 --> 00:04:59.660
So why did Riot do all this?

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:03.400
Obviously it’s impossible to say for sure, but it's safe to assume that Riot

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:08.500
wanted to bring up some bad champions, namely Darius, Gangplank and Mordekaiser,

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:13.640
And it's also safe to assume that they probably wanted to shake up the meta a little bit and make things exciting

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:14.800
going into Worlds.

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:18.660
"This is the first time before Worlds that there's been the most changes."

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:23.800
"Like Mordekaiser AD carry bottom lane like nobody in their right mind would say two years from now

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:28.620
I'm going to be playing against Mordekaiser bottom lane. You go in solo queue, Mordekaiser, Darius, Garen

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:32.340
and Skarner are banned, somebody's like, am I in an alternate universe, did I just wake up

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:33.540
am I still dreaming?"

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:38.040
Instead, teams chose to create a fragile peace, built on the understanding that the League

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:42.100
of Legends they were playing at Worlds 2015 wasn’t the League of Legends they qualified

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:43.340
for the tournament on.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:49.060
The teams at Worlds 2015 qualified through the summer split, on a patch without Juggernauts

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:51.160
and without an all-powerful Gangplank.

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:55.140
Riot changing the patch right before Worlds, meant that they just weren’t playing the

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:56.140
same game.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:59.540
SKT were the favorites to win the tournament and they did.

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:03.100
Of course it didn’t hurt that they were one of the only teams who brought a carry

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:07.940
top laner to a tpournament with plenty of carry top picks, but that’s not the point.

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:12.900
The point is that Riot changed the game, by most accounts for the worse,

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:15.940
just before their most important tournament of the year.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:20.440
It showed a complete lack of care for the competitive scene, and even though the

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:26.580
best team won out and most of the OP nonsense didn’t make it on stage, the tournament was fundamentally

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:28.580
changed because of Riot's decisions.

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:38.580
(Casting)

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:42.640
Worlds 2015 had the highest champion diversity of any Worlds up to that point.

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:47.120
But a lot of that was because most of the same champions were banned every single game.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:52.620
"Yeah and as for EDG's bans. I think since they're on red side they may just be locked into

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:54.940
the Gangplank, Mordekaiser and Elise."

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:59.820
In the bracket stage alone. Blue side had a 58.3 percent win rate.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:02.340
You weren't totally screwed on red side.

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:06.920
In fact SKT won two games on red side in their grand finals series against Koo Tigers.

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:09.340
But you were at a severe disadvantage.

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:13.680
These days, Riot is a lot more careful about letting their patches breathe before Worlds.

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:18.340
Towards the end of the tournament, Marc Merrill said that Riot was going to take a harder look at their patch

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:21.420
schedule around Worlds. And it seems like they've learned their lesson.

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:26.180
Worlds should be played on a stable patch, one that’s similar to the Summer Split.

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:27.760
It ensures competitive integrity.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:30.720
And that fans get to watch the best League of Legends possible.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:32.300
That the best teams win out.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:35.280
These days, Worlds is in a much healthier place.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:38.960
And that's probably worth one messy tournament.

